[Music]  hello there and welcome back you know  for a channel called monster maze I  think it's about high time we take a  look at an actual well maze the low male  labyrinths found in bref the wild a  total of three can be found across  Hyrule loam a labyrinth island Northland  a labyrinth and South loam a labyrinth  all of course built by the zone I  because it's ridiculous okay all kidding  aside though I'm not getting into the  lore behind these locations who built  them or why but rather look at these  landmarks on a mechanical level how they  function how they could be improved if  of course they make a return in the  sequel one fun little tidbit is that the  word loam a is actually an anagram for  the wards meadow meaning maze in  Japanese and in the English language  labyrinth is synonymous with maze so  basically these places are called maze  maze nice in actuality a maze and a  labyrinth are not the same thing  historically a labyrinth is a complex  pattern that only has one way to the  center without any branching and thus  there's no navigational challenge to  them however the terms are used  interchangeably nowadays when I first  stumbled across these massive landmarks  I was pleasantly surprised since it  stands out from the other shrine quests  found in the overworld if you've ever  seen The Maze Runner you know exactly  what I'm talking about  the idea of entering a large complex  maze having to map it out and find your  way around is an appealing thought so  let's see how these places fare against  some scrutiny shall we in my first draft  of this script I complained about the  aesthetics of these places it is very  bland and repetitive but then I figured  this isn't really a fair assessment it's  a maze after all the whole point of a  maze is to make you feel disoriented by  having each hallway and corridor look  the same it's harder to keep track of  where you've been and in this regard  these places definitely do succeed I  spent more time roaming these hallways  than I care to admit  each labyrinth has its own little quirk  lomi labyrinth island has a large  central room with a Guardian stalker  patrolling it and Guardian sky watchers  guarding the upper perimeter  additionally after you obtain the  barbarian helm from the shrine you gain  access to a basement section filled with  decayed Guardians some of which are  still active there's even one  incapacitated Guardian just laying  around one of the hallways north lomi  labyrinth is flipped 45 degrees on its  side and uses malice to halt your  progression it's also filled to the brim  with Rattlers elf lows and South Loma  labyrinths also has malice but there are  no enemies to fight inside only tuba  Carlin's are patrolling the outer wall  additionally it also makes use of a lot  of clothes off sections that cannot be  seen from above speaking of which I  think this is one of the aspects that  undermines the challenge a fair bit the  fact that you can see the structure of  the maze on your map while I was playing  through North lomi labyrinth I abuse  this quite a bit when you first enter it  you can immediately see the shrine  behind a set of bars the left path is  blocked by a significant amount of  malice first I explored all of these  hallways right here and got nowhere so I  pulled up my map and started connecting  the dots I know the shrine is right here  so I figured there has to be an entrance  to this room somewhere around this main  area since I already explored this  section the entrance has to be somewhere  around here  right so I climbed up to the top of the  maze which if you have enough stamina to  work with is actually pretty easy to do  and then from the top I navigated to the  location where I was guessing the  entrance had to be then I used the  paraglider to drop down and lo and  behold there it is in that regard south  lo mein labyrinth at least fares a  little bit better  since most of its corridors are  invisible from satellite view it doesn't  even really look like a maze on the map  as opposed to the other two I wondered  why not implement the same technique  they used with the sand storms in Gerudo  desert where your map function is  blocked as soon as you enter the area I  guess even the sheikah slate can deal  with bad reception of course you could  still cheat the system by prematurely  taking a picture with your phone before  you actually enter it but at least you  won't be able to see your current  location a different end /  HAP's more easy solution would be to  just have these structures show up as a  solid square or a rectangle on your map  now some people might actually enjoy the  fact that they have access to a map as  for me I would have rather used  different methods to navigate my way  through for instance by leaving a  breadcrumb trail to know which corridors  I already visited at the very least your  map should fill in as you progress  through in my opinion as for being able  to climb up and cheat the system just  well put a roof on it I get that they  wanted you to see this massive structure  from afar and be like wow what is that  putting a roof on it would take that  experience away to a certain extent in  that case they could have used something  else like a force field that activates  when you get closer to the maze if they  repeat something like this in breath the  wall - I think one of the better options  would be to have these kinds of  challenges underground one of my  favorite things from Twilight Princess  are these random caves that you can find  in the overworld  they branch out almost like the root of  a tree and some of them actually go on  pretty far last year I went to an  underground salt mine  together with my friend Conrad from the  Commonwealth realm and one of the most  exciting feelings was the fact that you  genuinely feel like you are in an  underground network of tunnels that lead  every which way if the lights go out  down here finding your way around would  be quite the challenge actually we have  seen darkness used as a tool to  disorient the player in divine beast  Varadhan yeah and even better ty flow  ruins by the way if you haven't seen  NBC's video on that topic yet what are  you doing with your life go watch it  it's amazing walking around in  pitch-black darkness with nothing but a  torch to light your way is a nice touch  maybe not as dark as in breath the wild  perhaps a little bit more draw distance  would be better now in terms of music I  think it's fitting that they reuse the  same track from the last woods after all  the original Japanese name for the Lost  Woods in the Legend of Zelda on NES was  maze woods coincidence  yes but fitting either way and now onto  the final aspect and perhaps one of the  most important ones at that when it  comes to exploration the rewards in the  form of loot and treasure the most  obvious reward is of course the three  pieces of the barbarian set which by  itself already makes it worth the effort  however as you saw  with my example of cheating north lomi  labyrinth you can totally ignore large  parts of the maze itself just to obtain  this piece of apparel it would be nice  to also get rewarded for exploring the  rest of the area and in this regard the  labyrinth definitely do not disappoint  North lomi labyrinth holds a total of  nine treasure chests as well as some  weapons shields and some luminous stones  that can be found just laying around  many of the chests hold fabulous rewards  most notably a diamond edge of duality  knight's shield gold and silver rupee  and three chests with topaz a sapphire  and a ruby one cool thing I also found  was this little campsite in one of the  hallways near the outer wall apparently  someone else already tried to find our  way through before you did and had to  set up camp when they got lost there's a  barrel a pile of wood used to make a  campfire and what seems to be the  decayed remains of a tent he or she even  set up this little flag as some sort of  a waypoint  pretty neat lomi labyrinth island is  also quite generous a total of eight  treasure chests and again some other  stuff just scattered around the rewards  are once again pretty good and makes it  worth the trouble of searching every  nook and cranny as opposed to just going  straight for the shrine as mentioned  before after beating the shrine itself  you gain access to an extra basement  level which rewards you with a diamond  circlet which is a fantastic reward  south Lommel labyrinth is a little less  generous but this is understandable as  it is the smallest and least complex it  houses a total of four treasure chests  holding a gold and silver rupee a garuda  shield and a Garuda scimitar there's  also a golden Claymore and bow sticking  out from a pool of malice it's great to  see that these locations reward  additional exploration all in all in  breath the wild aloma labyrinths granted  a nice change of pace when it came to  the shrine quests most of these quests  were tied to riddles the labyrinth stood  out together with tight flow ruins and  the survival trial uneven tied on event  I'd I have no idea how to pronounce the  name of that place other than that there  isn't much to say about the lomi  labyrinths except that they were cool  kinda some have suggested that perhaps  we will revisit them or are given a new  purpose in breath the wall to which yeah  I guess that could be a possibility  however they would have to add some  new to them like a new section on the  ground perhaps thanks a lot for watching  I hope the hours of me mindlessly  wandering these hallways brought you  some entertainment as a tease for the  next subject we will be looking further  into the aspect of darkness however this  time it's not gonna be a Zelda game but  rather a series that I also hold very  dear to me if you came to this channel  for the Zelda content fear not I will  still make comparisons between next  week's subject and 